              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "How long does Invisalign take?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Mild cases can be complete in 6 to 12 months. More involved cases with bite correction typically run 12 to 18 months. We give you a realistic timeline after reviewing your scan - not a number designed to sell you on starting."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Does Invisalign hurt?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "You'll feel pressure when you switch to a new aligner set, usually for a day or two. Most patients describe it as noticeable but manageable, and it fades quickly as your teeth adjust."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Can I eat and drink normally?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Yes. You remove the aligners before eating and drinking anything other than water. That also means your teeth are easier to keep clean during treatment - no food trapped around brackets."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "How many hours a day do I wear them?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "20 to 22 hours per day. The more consistently you wear them, the more predictably your teeth move. Skipping wear time is the most common reason cases run longer than planned."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Is Invisalign right for complex cases?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Invisalign can handle cases that once required braces. Whether it's right for your situation depends on your bite, gum health, and bone structure. We'll tell you honestly if braces would serve you better."
